  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 AM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nLooping the regional radar mosaic since last evening indicates \nthat the mid-level vort associated with an upper-level low was \ncentered just west-southwest of Chicago early this morning. This\nfeature appears to be becoming more sheared in response to a \nstronger mid- level short wave tracking southeast from southern \nIA into northeast MO and west-central IL. Isolated/widely \nscattered showers beneath the center of the original vort have \ndissipated over the last few hours, though have maintained some \ndecreasing slight chance (15-20%) pops near the center of the \ncirculation during the predawn hours in case anything \nredevelops. Can't completely rule out some isolated sprinkles or\neven patchy drizzle across the east/southeast parts of the cwa \nthis morning as the shearing vort continues to drift slowly off \nto the east-southeast, though rather shallow moisture profiles \nin forecast soundings indicate little substantial precip threat.\nExtensive (but shallow) low-level cloud deck was noted in GOES \nimagery spreading westward across the forecast area however, and\nthis should maintain cloudy/mostly cloudy skies across the area\ntoday, especially across the southern half/two thirds of the \narea. Cloud cover and still-breezy northeast winds will keep \ntemperatures on the cool side once again, with highs in the \nmid-60s for most areas. \n\nWith our mid-level short-wave energy slowly tracking off to the\nsoutheast of the region though the end of the week, we'll see \nupper ridging develop into the western Great Lakes region \n(though tempered by the passage of a mid-level short wave along \nthe U.S.Canadian border Thursday night into Friday). This will \nallow the western periphery of the elongated surface ridge to \nour north settle across the area, and will maintain dry and \nseasonably cool weather conditions along with diminishing winds \nfor the local area.\n\nEvolution of a deep cut-off low along the east coast by the \nweekend will tend to set up a blocky upper-air pattern across \nthe eastern CONUS, preventing much in the way of significant \nprecipitation chances for the forecast area. Guidance does \ndepict another more amplified short-wave propagating into the \nupper Midwest this weekend, with the potential for a (likely) \ndecaying area of precip to approach the forecast area late \nSaturday night/early Sunday. This wave will be pushing into the \nupper ridge and fighting drier low- level air, however. Global \nensemble pops remain focused the Mississippi River through the \nweekend. The NBM initialized with some low-chance pops across \nour western cwa late Saturday night/early and made no changes, \nthough it doesn't look like a significant rain threat for the \ndaytime weekend hours. A gradual modest warm-up back to average \ntemps (low/mid 70s) is expected later in the weekend into early \nnext week. Extended guidance/ensembles eventually eject the deep\neast coast cut-off low off to the northeast Monday/Tuesday, \nwith the next chance of any significant precip likely holding \noff until the middle of next week.\n\nRatzer",
